"A PKI certificate chain diagram connects a subject certificate through issuers to a configured trust anchor.\n\nUse when: Explain certificate path validation and intermediate certificates. Investigate a trust failure without exposing private key material.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is an OAuth exchange or secret-delivery workflow.\n\nVisual grammar: Certificate cards show subject, issuer, and selected public constraints. Signed-by edges distinguish issuance direction from validation traversal. Trust-anchor markers identify local trust configuration rather than an intrinsic certificate property.\n\nBuild: Choose a certificate path and capture only public metadata. Place leaf, intermediate issuers, and trust anchor in order. Annotate validity, key usage, and path constraints relevant to the question. Route verification notes beside the chain with ample space between certificate cards.\n\nCheck: Issuer relationships match signatures and identities. A root is trusted only because the relying party configures trust. Validity, revocation, and name checks are not omitted from validation claims. Chain arrows and metadata text do not intersect.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a supplied root certificate automatically establishes trust. Confusing public certificate information with private signing keys.\n\nExample: api.example.test presents a leaf and intermediate; the client builds a path to its configured root anchor.\n\nAlternatives: Identity federation diagram, Merkle tree."
